+## 2Q Cache Eviction
+
+Use a 2Q (Two Queue) eviction policy to protect repeatedly-used comparison sheets from being evicted during long sequential screenings. The cache maintains two LRU queues:
+
+- **A1 (first-use queue)**: Holds previews accessed exactly once. New cache admissions enter here. Sized at 25% of the total cache capacity (3 GiB).
+- **Am (main queue)**: Holds previews accessed more than once. Sized at 75% of the total cache capacity (9 GiB).
+
+When a preview in A1 is accessed again, it is promoted to Am. This promotion indicates the preview is part of an active comparison set rather than a one-time screening view.
+
+When admitting a new preview would exceed capacity:
+1. If A1 is at capacity, evict the least-recently-used entry from A1.
+2. If A1 has space but the total cache would exceed 12 GiB, evict the least-recently-used entry from Am.
+3. Admit the new preview to A1.
+
+On a cache hit:
+- If the preview is in A1 and this is its second access, move it from A1 to Am (promoting it).
+- If the preview is in Am, update its position to most-recently-used within Am.
+- If promoting from A1 to Am would cause Am to exceed its 9 GiB limit, evict the least-recently-used entry from Am first.
+
+The 25%/75% split protects the main working set (Am) from being flushed by sequential scans while still admitting enough one-time views to serve screening workflows. All queue membership and LRU position tracking remains internal to the preview worker.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Cache eviction policy | 2Q (two-queue LRU variant) | LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging | See [decisions/cache-eviction-policy.md](decisions/cache-eviction-policy.md). 2Q protects comparison sheets from sequential-scan eviction while maintaining bounded overhead. |
+| A1/Am queue size split | 25% A1 (3 GiB), 75% Am (9 GiB) | 50%/50%, 10%/90% | 25%/75% balances screening and comparison workflows. A larger A1 would sacrifice too much protection for the comparison set; a smaller A1 would cause legitimate second accesses to evict main-queue entries too frequently. This split is a starting point and may be tuned based on observed workload. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Deferred
+1. Whether the 25%/75% queue split should be tunable per deployment or remain fixed for the pilot.
+2. Whether to log promotion events for later workload analysis.
